Title: PL/pgSQL SQL Procedural Language
Section: PL/pgSQL SQL Procedural Language > Declaring Function Parameters

Output parameters are most useful when returning multiple values.
A trivial example is: CREATE FUNCTION sum_n_product(x int, y int, OUT sum int, OUT prod int) AS $$
BEGIN
sum := x + y;
prod := x * y;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

SELECT * FROM sum_n_product(2, 4);
sum | prod
-----+------
6 | 8 As discussed in , this
effectively creates an anonymous record type for the function's
results. If a RETURNS clause is given, it must say RETURNS record .

```sql
CREATE FUNCTION sum_n_product(x int, y int, OUT sum int, OUT prod int) AS $$
BEGIN
    sum := x + y;
    prod := x * y;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

SELECT * FROM sum_n_product(2, 4);
 sum | prod
-----+------
   6 |    8
```

